Session 12 (2026-07-23) — follow-up probes to the FAILED clipk pivot check.
Both are verbatim outputs of inline python drivers around fit_nonlinear.render_profiles()
(same HELD set as the fit; OfflineRender with the session-12 clipK-capable build).

PROBE 1 — which harmonic moves at noon? Absolute Hn re-fund (dB), session-11 fitted
point [s=0.24601, a=2.6099, cp=0.48727, cn=0.27357, A0=29.937, satLo=1.2328, satHi=1.5779]:

      k |  noon H2  noon H3  noon H4 |   min H2   min H3
   4.00 |    -32.0    -49.8    -63.1 |    -40.9    -58.6
   2.00 |    -32.1    -51.3    -61.1 |    -40.9    -58.7
   1.25 |    -32.5    -56.0    -58.1 |    -41.0    -59.3

-> H2 is ~static; the TOTAL H3 FALLS as k softens, opposite to the clipper-alone probe
   (which shows the clipper's own H3 rising). H4 rises as H3 falls. Interference suspected.

PROBE 2 — same sweep with the JFET drain-current ceiling DISABLED (cp = cn = 1e6,
everything else at the fitted point):

      k |  noon H2  noon H3 |   min H3
   4.00 |    -33.2    -85.9 |   -140.4
   2.00 |    -33.4    -64.8 |    -98.2
   1.25 |    -33.9    -54.6 |    -80.8

-> With the ceiling off, noon H3 RISES monotonically as k drops (+31.3 dB over the sweep),
   restoring the clipper-alone behaviour exactly. So with the ceiling ON, the JFET
   ceiling's drive-independent H3 (~-49.8 at noon, the dominant source there) and the
   clipper's H3 are ANTI-PHASE: as the clipper's H3 grows toward parity it CANCELS the
   total. Quantitative check at k=1.25: |10^(-49.8/20) - 10^(-54.6/20)| -> -57.2 dB
   predicted vs -56.0 dB measured (near-180-degree phase). INTERFERENCE CONFIRMED.

Consequence: the session-11 diagnosis ("noon deficit = clipper knee hardness") is WRONG
as stated — hardness k cannot raise noon H3-H2 in the full chain (it lowers it), and it
wrecks 2:30 (+0.8 -> +21.1 dB at k=1.25). See step4b_clipk_pivot.log (nominal point) and
step4b_clipk_pivot_fittedpoint.log (fitted point): the pivot signature FAILED at both.
STOPPED at the section-3j gate before any fit, per the agreed protocol.
